<article class="env-definition" id="04FZ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04FZ">Definition <span data-tag="04FZ">1.3.2.1</span>.</a> A <em>monoid</em> is a set $M$ equipped with a multiplication map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[ m: M \times M \rightarrow M \quad \quad (x,y) \mapsto xy \]
</div>
<p> which satisfies the following conditions: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a)$</dt>
<dd><p>
The multiplication $m$ is associative. That is, we have $x(yz) = (xy)z$ for each triple of elements $x,y,z \in M$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b)$</dt>
<dd><p>
There exists an element $e \in M$ such that $ex=x=xe$ for each $x \in M$ (in this case, the element $e$ is uniquely determined; we refer to it as the <em>unit element</em> of $M$). 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
</article>
